WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.040
Complex tasks are often easier when divided into smaller stages.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:10.100
A document workflow might first extract facts, then organize them by theme, then draft a final summary.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.480
A software workflow might identify requirements, propose test cases, and then draft implementation notes.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:22.940
A research assistance workflow might gather source-supported claims, compare them, and then produce a brief with citations.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.720
Decomposition turns one large ambiguous request into smaller reviewable steps.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.500
Smaller tasks are easier to specify.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.000
Intermediate outputs can be inspected before they are used.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.060
Errors may be easier to locate.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.560
Different steps may use different prompts, tools, retrieval sources, or models.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:48.580
For example, an extraction step may require strict labeled fields, while a drafting step may require readable prose.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.680
Separating those steps can improve reliability and make failures easier to diagnose.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.140
Multi-step workflows have tradeoffs.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.320
More model calls can increase cost and latency.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:05.140
Errors can propagate between stages if an early extraction mistake becomes input to later drafting.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.120
Intermediate data may include sensitive content that needs protection.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:15.180
Workflow state, retries, caching, logging, and approvals become application concerns.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.960
Decomposition helps when it improves control, not merely because it adds steps.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.420
Iterative prompting should also be controlled.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.920
Test the prompt.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.420
Inspect failures.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.640
Revise one important factor.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:28.820
Retest.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.560
Record what changed.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:36.140
Randomly adding instructions after every disappointing output can make the prompt longer and less reliable.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:46.200
A disciplined iteration cycle treats prompts as versioned components: define the expected behavior, test representative cases, change deliberately, and compare results.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.980
A practical iteration log can be simple.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.840
Record the prompt version, the reason for the change, the test cases affected, and the observed improvement or regression.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:02.380
If a summarization prompt starts omitting caveats, add a requirement for uncertainty handling and rerun the same cases.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:08.280
If a classification prompt confuses two labels, improve the label definitions or add examples.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:12.180
The goal is evidence-driven improvement, not prompt folklore.
